<EngineeringUpdate />

## What the export showed

Do not close new reports as one known date bug. The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471)
merged reports from different paths and incompatible symptoms. Track each problem independently:

- **Parser:** [parser ticket](https://github.com/tayiorbeii/mega-s01e03/issues/1).
  The shared parser reads month first for day-first tenants. An app/email booking for
  7 April can become 4 July. Look up the tenant's locale; browser locale is not authoritative.
- **Hint:** [booking-v3 hint ticket](https://github.com/tayiorbeii/mega-s01e03/issues/2).
  For day-first tenants on booking-v3, the form prints MM/DD/YYYY but reads in the
  tenant's order. A person following that hint can get a swapped booking. Fix the hint/input
  agreement separately; fixing the shared parser alone does not establish that this is fixed.
- **One-day/date-boundary shifts:** [separate investigation ticket](https://github.com/tayiorbeii/mega-s01e03/issues/3).
  These reports do not describe month/day swaps. Their problem is independently actionable
  even though this export does not establish a timezone or other root cause.

## Computed ticket counts

<FindingTable />

Counts preserve the desk view as exported: deduplicate by report ID, then use the tenant-aware
classifier for parser and hint. The five reports flagged in desk notes also get their own
investigation ticket, but remain included under parser in the exported count. These are
overlapping views, not a partition: adding ticket counts does not give the unique-report total.
Intake tags are guesses based on browser locale, not the issue counts.

## The desk view, as exported

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

This table supplies the desk's cause counts, including the five one-day reports filed under
parser. causes.ts looks only at channel and tenant settings, not report bodies. Its two
groups do not remove the need to investigate the separate timing symptoms.

<ReportAudit />

## The queue did not stop when the desk merged it

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April onward, but merging began in July. Received dates measure when
people reported trouble; merged dates measure desk processing. The July merge burst is not
proof of a July onset or a sudden spike in new failures. Reports continue through October;
known status does not establish a shipped fix. Refresh the export before claiming live counts.

## Replace subject-only triage

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is historical evidence, not a safe
rule to keep following. Its accepted status does not make its stale text correct.
[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) replaces it without changing that record.

<DedupeExplorer />

The same subject can hide different causes; different subjects or channels can share one.
Use tenant locale, form version, channel and the report body. Unknown tenants and unexplained
symptoms stay separate pending investigation. Read the body's contradictions even when the
current classifier assigns a cause. The five one-day reports have their own investigation
ticket while remaining included in the parser count as exported. State that overlap rather
than changing the desk's counts. A separate problem does not require pretending we know
its root cause. Deduplicate IDs when reporting the overall number of reports.

## Verification and limits

This page is rendered from the actual .svx and supplied Svelte components; its tables, chart,
examples and desk ref are computed, not copied from the old prose. The source export contains
no production fix or current desk connection. Issues document problems and acceptance tests;
the engineering update above records the subsequent desk-reported outcomes. The rendered HTML is a static snapshot: use the source app for
interactive triage and rebuild after data changes.

